<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04ZH">Example <span data-tag="04ZH">1.1.1.8</span>.</a> Let $S_{\bullet }$ be a simplicial set and let $\sigma $ be a $2$-simplex of $S_{\bullet }$. Then $\sigma $ has three faces: the edges $f = d^{2}_{2}(\sigma )$, $g = d^{2}_{0}(\sigma )$, and $h = d^{2}_{1}(\sigma )$. In this case, Remark <a data-tag="04FP" href="/tag/04FP">1.1.1.7</a> asserts the following: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The edges $f$ and $h$ have the same source vertex $x \in S_{\bullet }$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The edges $g$ and $h$ have the same target vertex $z \in S_{\bullet }$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The target of $f$ and the source of $g$ are the same vertex $y \in S_{\bullet }$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p> These relationships can be encoded visually in the diagram </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@C =50pt@R=50pt{ &amp;  y \ar [dr]^{g} &amp;  \\ x \ar [ur]^{f} \ar [rr]^{h} &amp;  &amp;  z. } \]
</div>
</article>
